The striking thing I think about that moment was, in a way, it's part of these islands' history, isn't it? Boats always being trouble. Whether it's Vikings or more latterly, people coming across the channel. The English are always worried about boats turning up. But they also bring new beginnings. So whether it's Julius Caesar landing or actually more germainly, the Anglo-Saxons, you know, Hengist and Horsa landing at Thanet. I mean, it is a narrative of invasion, but it's also a narrative of new beginnings and new opportunities. So, I mean, that is such a fascinating point. I think we should kind of look at that perhaps towards the end of the show when we're looking at how the resonance that the Windrush has.
我认为那个时刻最引人注目的是，在某种程度上，它是这些岛屿历史的一部分，不是吗？船只总是带来麻烦。无论是维京人，还是最近穿过海峡的人。英国人总是担心船只的出现。但它们也带来了新的开始。所以无论是尤利乌斯·凯撒的登陆，还是更贴切地说，盎格鲁-撒克逊人，你知道，亨吉斯特（Hengist）和霍萨（Horsa）在萨尼特（Thanet）的登陆。我的意思是，这是一个入侵的叙事，但它也是一个关于新开始和新机遇的叙事。所以，我的意思是，这是一个非常迷人的观点。我想我们应该在节目快结束时探讨一下，当我们审视“疾风号”所产生的共鸣时。

